## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend streaming and batch processing with a practical ML feature store, including contracts, versioning, lineage, and schema evolution—common in large tech orgs.

## Key Concepts
- Lakehouse (Iceberg) with feature tables
- Versioned feature registry and per-record data contracts
- Real-time (Flink) vs batch (Spark) feature computation
- Idempotent upserts and late-data handling
- End-to-end data lineage and monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
function validateRecord(record, schema) {
  // naive contract: required keys and types
  for (const [k, t] of Object.entries(schema.fields)) {
    if (!(k in record)) return { ok: false, error: `missing ${k}` };
    if (typeof record[k] !== t) return { ok: false, error: `${k} type` };
  }
  return { ok: true };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you isolate feature namespaces for different teams and enforce RBAC?
- How would you test schema evolution with backward-compat migrations?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical data quality, late data handling, and observability in a real-world lakehouse pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Per-record contracts and simple validator logic
- Upserts into Iceberg star schema with deduplication by event_id
- Late data handling with watermarking (15 minutes)
- Lightweight observability: lineage, conforming vs non-conforming counts, metrics sink
- Trade-offs: Spark Structured Streaming vs Flink for beginner workloads

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo validator
function validate(record) {
  if (!record.event_id || !record.ts || !record.device_id) return false;
  if (typeof record.metrics?.value !== 'number') return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you design the bad_data retention and purge policy?
- Which metrics would you expose to operators for quick health checks?
